  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Essential Services for Maternal and Child Health</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="FR">Services Essentiels de Sant&#xe9; &#x200b;Maternelle et Infantile (SEMI)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Maternal mortality is one of the best indicators of health system development and social inequalities in health care access. The DRC ranks in the top ten worst countries for maternal mortality, estimated at 473 maternal deaths per 100,000 live births in 2017. Under-five mortality in DRC is also among the highest at 78 per 1,000 live births, of which 46% are neonatal deaths. These statistics underscore the challenges that DRC continues to face at a very basic level.  Recognizing FCDO&#x2019;s leadership in bringing positive change for women and girls worldwide and their commitment to health systems strengthening in DRC, through the SEMI project, IMA is tackling preventable deaths of mothers and babies as the linchpin to developing the health system and addressing social inequalities in access to health care in Kasa&#xef; province. The DRC remains burdened by a health system with a limited capacity to reduce high maternal, newborn and child mortality, decrease chronic malnutrition and respond to outbreaks. SEMI&#x2019;s interventions focus on:A.Improving access to essential maternal, new born, child &amp; adolescent health services;B.Promoting rights-based family planning services for women and girls; andC.Strengthening the health system in the following areas: - Community governance mechanisms- Human resources for health- Drug supply chain management- Health information system quality and completeness- Public finance management at the central level</narrative>
  </description>
